        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'Assembling the propulsion requirement:\n\n  MAXIMUM SPEED    -> sets POWER available at high speed\n                      (drag dominates, P ~ V^3)\n\n  GRADEABILITY     -> sets CONTINUOUS torque at low speed\n                      (thermally limited, sustained)\n\n  ACCELERATION     -> sets PEAK torque at low speed\n                      (brief, thermally tolerable)\n\n  ADHESION LIMIT   -> CAPS the usable peak torque\n                      (beyond it: wheelspin, no benefit)\n\nSelect the motor envelope to enclose all three points.\n\nBeware oversizing: extra motor mass worsens EVERY\nrequirement it was meant to satisfy.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.2, pp.45-57.' },
        mistake: 'Students size the motor on acceleration alone because it is the most visible specification. Sustained gradeability usually sets the continuous rating and therefore the thermal design, and a motor sized only for a brief acceleration burst will overheat on a long climb.',
