        terms: ['range', 'usable capacity', 'depth of discharge', 'mass spiral', 'diminishing returns'],
        definition:
          'Range is usable battery energy divided by consumption per kilometre, where usable energy is less than nominal capacity because operating between full and empty shortens battery life. Sizing a battery for long range runs into a spiral: a larger battery adds mass, which raises rolling resistance and the energy needed for acceleration, which raises consumption, which requires still more battery for a given range. The returns therefore diminish, and beyond a certain point additional capacity adds mass almost as fast as it adds energy.',
        takeaway: 'More battery adds mass which adds consumption which needs more battery — the returns diminish.',
        visual: 'mass-spiral',
        visualSpec:
          'A feedback loop diagram: larger battery leads to greater mass, leads to higher rolling resistance and acceleration energy, leads to higher consumption, leads back to requiring a larger battery. Beside it, a range against battery capacity plot shows the curve rising but bending over, compared with a straight line showing what range would be if mass did not increase, the gap between them widening. A usable capacity annotation shows nominal capacity reduced by a reserved margin at each end for battery life. On reveal: the loop diagram draws arrow by arrow closing on itself, then the range plot draws its straight ideal line, then the actual curve bends away beneath it with the gap shading, then the usable capacity annotation trims the axis.',
        algo: [
          'Determine the required range and the consumption per kilometre',
          'Compute the usable energy required as their product',
          'Convert to nominal capacity by allowing for the reserved depth of discharge margin',
          'Add the resulting battery mass to the vehicle mass',
          'Recompute consumption with the new mass and iterate until it converges',
        ],
        complexity: { best: 'a modest range target converges in one or two iterations', avg: 'the iteration is needed because mass and consumption are coupled', worst: 'a very long range target may not converge to a sensible vehicle at all', note: 'The spiral is why very long range vehicles are disproportionately heavy and expensive.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'Doubling battery capacity on a vehicle to double its range.',
          steps: [
            'Doubling capacity doubles battery mass, adding perhaps 300 kg to a 1600 kg vehicle',
            'That is a 19 percent mass increase, raising rolling resistance and acceleration energy proportionally',
            'Consumption rises by perhaps 10 percent overall, since drag is unaffected',
            'Range therefore rises by about 82 percent rather than 100 percent',
          ],
          result: 'Less than double the range for double the battery. The shortfall grows with each further doubling, which is the diminishing return that limits practical range.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'Range:\n\n  range = usable battery energy / consumption per km\n\n  usable < nominal: a margin is reserved at BOTH ends\n  (full and empty both shorten battery life)\n\nTHE MASS SPIRAL:\n\n  bigger battery -> more MASS\n                 -> more rolling resistance\n                    + more acceleration energy\n                 -> higher CONSUMPTION\n                 -> need an even bigger battery\n\n  -> DIMINISHING RETURNS\n\n  Note drag is UNAFFECTED by mass, so the spiral is\n  weaker at high speed and strongest in urban driving.\n\nSizing requires ITERATION until mass and consumption\nconverge.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.4, pp.112-116.' },
        mistake: 'Students compute range by dividing nominal capacity by consumption for the original vehicle mass. The added battery mass raises consumption, so the calculation must be iterated, and neglecting this overstates range.',
