        definition:
          'A direct current machine has a stationary field system producing flux and a rotating armature carrying current in that flux, so a force is produced on every conductor and the sum of those forces gives torque. For the torque to remain in one direction as the armature rotates, the current in each conductor must reverse as it passes from one pole to the next, and the commutator and brushes perform exactly that reversal mechanically. The commutator is therefore a mechanical inverter, converting the direct current supplied at the brushes into alternating current in the armature conductors.',
        takeaway: 'The commutator is a mechanical inverter — it reverses conductor current so the torque stays one way.',
