        definition:
          'Practical drives use both methods in sequence to obtain the widest useful envelope. From standstill the field is held at full value and the armature voltage is raised, giving constant torque up to base speed where the voltage reaches its maximum. Beyond that the voltage is held at maximum and the field is weakened, giving constant power up to the commutation limit. The controller therefore switches strategy at base speed, and the resulting envelope is the direct current drive equivalent of the traction characteristic identified in Module 2.',
