        definition:
          'A brushless direct current machine inverts the construction of a conventional direct current machine: the flux producing magnets are placed on the rotor and the current carrying winding on the stator. That inversion removes the need for a commutator, but the function the commutator performed must still be carried out, so the winding currents are switched electronically according to the measured or estimated rotor position. The machine is therefore a direct current machine in its characteristics and an alternating current machine in its physical operation, which is why its name causes so much confusion.',
