        slides: [29, 30, 31],
        terms: ['doubly salient', 'no rotor winding', 'no magnets', 'phase winding', 'aligned and unaligned'],
        definition:
          'A switched reluctance machine is doubly salient, meaning both stator and rotor have projecting poles, and its rotor is simply a shaped lump of laminated steel with no windings, no magnets and no conductors of any kind. Concentrated coils on the stator poles are grouped into phases. The rotor has fewer poles than the stator, so for any rotor position some rotor poles are aligned with an energised stator phase and others are not, and the machine works by exploiting the difference between those two conditions.',
